SOURCE
id: 1
ts: 2026-05-29T10:00:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: pyflakes F821 - Undefined Name
authors: PyCQA
year: 2024
venue: pyflakes (PyCQA)
quality: CODE
url: https://www.flake8rules.com/rules/F821.html
keywords: undefined-name, unbound variable, scope analysis, name resolution
cites:
cited_by:
content:
  RULE ID: F821 (pyflakes), re-emitted as F821 in flake8
  WHAT IT FLAGS: Reference to a name that is not defined anywhere in the accessible scope at
    module or function level. Catches global undefined names. Does NOT catch the
    use-before-assignment case for locals (that is F823). Distinguishes undefined (never
    defined in scope) from possibly-unbound (defined on some paths only).
  FLOW PROPERTY: Scope-level name binding analysis. Pyflakes walks the AST building a
    Scope object (FunctionScope, ModuleScope, ClassScope) tracking all Binding and
    NameBinding objects. A USE of a name with no corresponding Binding in any enclosing
    scope triggers F821. This is NOT a full CFG/reaching-definitions analysis — it is
    scope-level binding presence check only.
  IMPLEMENTATION: pyflakes/checker.py — Checker class builds a scope stack via
    visitFunctionDef, visitClassDef etc.; Binding subclasses (Importation,
    Argument, FunctionDefinition, NameBinding) are registered on scope entry;
    after scope completes, unused and undefined bindings are reported.
  FALSE POSITIVES: Dynamic attribute injection (setattr, __dict__ manipulation),
    __all__ exports, star imports (from mod import *) prevent pyflakes from
    resolving names — common source of F821 false positives. IPython magic
    expressions also cause false positives (GitHub issue #786).
  CLASSIFICATION: AST-LINT (scope binding presence, not CFG-based). No reaching
    definitions traversal — a name either exists in scope or it doesn't.
  TOOLS: pyflakes (primary), flake8 (via pyflakes plugin), ruff (re-implements as F821)
<<<END>>>

SOURCE
id: 2
ts: 2026-05-29T10:01:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: pyflakes F823 - Local Variable Referenced Before Assignment
authors: PyCQA
year: 2024
venue: pyflakes (PyCQA)
quality: CODE
url: https://www.flake8rules.com/rules/F823.html
keywords: local variable, referenced before assignment, unbound, use-before-def
cites:
cited_by:
content:
  RULE ID: F823 (pyflakes) / E0601 used-before-assignment (pylint)
  WHAT IT FLAGS: A local variable (one that is assigned anywhere in the function body)
    is READ before any assignment to it. Python's scoping rules make any variable
    assigned in a function body a local; reading it before the assignment causes
    UnboundLocalError at runtime. Classic case: variable is conditionally assigned
    in one branch but read unconditionally before the branch.
  FLOW PROPERTY: Requires intra-procedural reaching-definitions or dominance analysis:
    "every path from function entry to the USE must pass through a DEF." Pyflakes'
    implementation is scope-only: it detects the trivial case where a variable is
    assigned after its first use in linear code. The "possibly-unbound" variant
    (conditional branches, try/except, finally) is detected separately as a warning
    (not F823) by pyflakes and more rigorously by pyright/mypy.
  SPECIAL CASE — finally/except: Variable assigned in try-body, read in finally:
    if the try raises before the assignment, the read in finally will fail with
    UnboundLocalError. This is the hardest variant to detect. Pyflakes issue #236 /
    mypy issue #17387 track this gap. Pyright has reportPossiblyUnbound for it.
  SPECIAL CASE — except variable erasure (Python 3): In Python 3, the `as e` name
    in `except Exception as e` is deleted after the except block. Reading `e` after
    the block raises NameError. Pyflakes issue #345 tracks detection of this case.
  FALSE POSITIVES: exec/eval-based dynamic assignment, walrus operator inside
    compound expressions (issue #3275 in pylint), type annotation-only lines.
  CLASSIFICATION: FLOW-NATIVE (requires reaching definitions or dominator analysis
    to handle the conditional/branching case correctly).
  TOOLS: pyflakes (F823, limited to linear case), pylint (E0601 used-before-assignment,
    more CFG-aware), pyright (reportUnboundVariable / reportPossiblyUnbound),
    mypy (--enable-error-code possibly-undefined), CodeQL (py/uninitialized-local-variable)
<<<END>>>

SOURCE
id: 3
ts: 2026-05-29T10:02:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: pyright reportPossiblyUnbound / reportUnboundVariable
authors: Microsoft (Eric Traut)
year: 2024
venue: microsoft/pyright GitHub
quality: DOCS
url: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/microsoft/pyright/main/docs/configuration.md
keywords: possibly-unbound, unbound variable, type narrowing, dataflow, pyright, pylance
cites:
cited_by:
content:
  RULE IDs:
    reportUnboundVariable — "Generate or suppress diagnostics for unbound variables."
      Default: "error" in Basic mode and above.
    reportPossiblyUnboundVariable — "Generate or suppress diagnostics for variables that
      are possibly unbound on some code paths." Default: "error" in Standard mode and above.
  WHAT THEY FLAG:
    reportUnboundVariable: variable is NEVER assigned before use on any path.
    reportPossiblyUnboundVariable: variable is assigned on SOME but not ALL paths leading
      to the use. Triggers on: variables defined only inside if/elif branches without
      else, variables assigned in try but read in finally (before assignment executes),
      variables defined in for/while body but read after loop (loop might not execute).
  FLOW PROPERTY: Full intra-procedural dataflow analysis with type narrowing. Pyright
    builds a CFG and propagates type state (including binding state) through join nodes.
    At a join point after a conditional, a variable's state is the INTERSECTION of what
    is known on each incoming edge. If any incoming edge has the variable unbound, the
    post-join state is "possibly-unbound."
  FALSE POSITIVES DOCUMENTED: 
    - Variables assigned in with-blocks sometimes reported as possibly-unbound in certain
      context managers (GitHub issue #7009). 
    - for-loops "that run at least once" — pyright conservatively treats the loop body as
      potentially not executing (discussion #2033).
    - Exhaustive elif chains without explicit else — pyright may not recognize exhaustive
      coverage via isinstance or Literal narrowing (issue #7396).
    - pd.DataFrame isinstance narrowing not recognized (issue #2540).
  CLASSIFICATION: FLOW-NATIVE. Pyright is the most CFG-complete of the commonly used
    Python linters for this detector.
  RELATED RULES:
    reportOptionalSubscript, reportOptionalMemberAccess, reportOptionalCall,
    reportOptionalIterable, reportOptionalContextManager, reportOptionalOperand —
    all default to "error" in Basic mode; catch None-dereference patterns on Optional-typed
    variables.
  TOOLS: pyright (primary), pylance (VS Code extension built on pyright)
<<<END>>>

