id: 5
ts: 2026-05-29T10:04:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: pylint E0606 possibly-used-before-assignment
authors: pylint-dev
year: 2024
venue: pylint readthedocs
quality: DOCS
url: https://pylint.pycqa.org/en/stable/user_guide/messages/error/possibly-used-before-assignment.html
keywords: possibly-used-before-assignment, conditional assignment, unbound, E0606
cites:
cited_by:
content:
  RULE ID: E0606
  MESSAGE TEXT: "Possibly using variable %r before assignment"
  WHAT IT FLAGS: A local variable is accessed before its assignment in at least one
    branch of conditional logic (if/else). Detects: variable assigned only inside
    an if-block but referenced after the conditional; variables assigned in some
    branches of if/else but not all; missing initialization before conditional.
  FLOW PROPERTY: Control flow path analysis — must verify that a variable has a
    definition on EVERY possible execution path leading to its use. Requires join-point
    analysis: if any incoming CFG edge lacks a definition, the variable is "possibly
    unbound" at the join point.
  FIX PATTERN: Initialize variable before conditional block to ensure definition on
    all code paths.
  CLASSIFICATION: FLOW-NATIVE (requires CFG join-point analysis)
  TOOLS: pylint (E0606)
<<<END>>>

SOURCE
id: 6
ts: 2026-05-29T10:05:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: pylint W0101 unreachable
authors: pylint-dev
year: 2024
venue: pylint readthedocs
quality: DOCS
url: https://pylint.pycqa.org/en/stable/user_guide/messages/warning/unreachable.html
keywords: unreachable code, dead code, W0101, return, raise, pylint
cites:
cited_by:
content:
  RULE ID: W0101 (unreachable)
  WHAT IT FLAGS: Code that appears after a return or raise statement in the same block,
    making it impossible to execute. Created by pylint's basic checker.
    Example: `return True; print("Hello")` — the print is flagged.
  FLOW PROPERTY: Simple intra-block post-terminator detection. After encountering a
    return/raise in a basic block, any subsequent statement in the same block is unreachable.
    This is a SHALLOW CFG property: it does not analyze unreachability through conditional
    branches or multiple basic blocks — it only catches direct post-return/raise statements
    within the same compound statement.
  SCOPE: Fires on code after return or raise. Does NOT fire on dead code that is
    unreachable because of constant conditions (that would require constant propagation).
    Does NOT cover break/continue unreachability between blocks.
  FALSE POSITIVES: Minimal for the simple case. Does not flag generator-style patterns
    that use raise StopIteration after yield (issue #8909 tracks false-positive for
    error-emitting generators).
  CLASSIFICATION: FLOW-NATIVE (basic CFG reachability — shallow variant)
  TOOLS: pylint (W0101); CodeQL (py/unreachable-statement, more comprehensive);
    vulture (detects unreachable code as part of dead code analysis)
<<<END>>>

