id: 8
ts: 2026-05-29T10:07:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: pylint W0631 undefined-loop-variable
authors: pylint-dev
year: 2024
venue: pylint readthedocs
quality: DOCS
url: https://pylint.readthedocs.io/en/stable/user_guide/messages/warning/undefined-loop-variable.html
keywords: undefined-loop-variable, loop variable, for loop, post-loop use, W0631
cites:
cited_by:
content:
  RULE ID: W0631 (undefined-loop-variable)
  WHAT IT FLAGS: A loop variable (defined by a for loop, list comprehension, or generator
    expression) is used outside/after the loop. If the iterable is empty, the loop body
    never executes, the variable is never bound, and the post-loop use raises NameError.
  FLOW PROPERTY: Requires knowing that: (1) a name is bound ONLY in a for-loop target,
    (2) the loop might not execute (empty iterable), (3) the name is used after the loop
    exits. This is a specific form of "possibly-unbound" analysis where the definition
    is guarded by loop execution. Requires reaching-definitions with loop-path awareness:
    the def ONLY reaches post-loop code if the loop body executes at least once.
  FALSE POSITIVES DOCUMENTED:
    - For loops with a function call that returns NoReturn (issue #7311) — pylint
      does not recognize NoReturn semantics, causing false positives.
    - Lambda statements inside first of two loops (issue #6419).
    - When the loop iterates over a known non-empty collection (pylint cannot always
      prove non-emptiness).
  CLASSIFICATION: FLOW-NATIVE (reaching definitions + loop-path analysis)
  TOOLS: pylint (W0631); pyright (reportPossiblyUnbound covers this case);
    mypy (possibly-undefined covers this case)
<<<END>>>

SOURCE
id: 9
ts: 2026-05-29T10:08:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: pylint R1710 inconsistent-return-statements
authors: pylint-dev
year: 2024
venue: pylint readthedocs
quality: DOCS
url: https://pylint.readthedocs.io/en/stable/user_guide/messages/refactor/inconsistent-return-statements.html
keywords: inconsistent-return, missing return, R1710, CFG, all paths
cites:
cited_by:
content:
  RULE ID: R1710
  WHAT IT FLAGS: A function has at least one code path that falls off the end (implicit
    None return) while other paths have explicit return value. Two sub-cases:
    (a) some branches return a value, other branches have bare return or fall-through
    (b) every path must end in a return statement but does not
  FLOW PROPERTY: Full intra-procedural CFG traversal. Must identify ALL exit points
    (return statements, implicit fall-through at end of function) and check whether they
    are consistent. Requires: building function CFG, finding all terminal nodes (nodes
    with no successors within the function), checking whether each terminal either has
    an explicit return-with-value or is a raise/NoReturn call.
  CFG IMPLEMENTATION DETAIL: A custom checker for inconsistent returns constructs a
    CFG from the function to check for inconsistent returns if there are multiple return
    statements, and missing return statements if there are none. (Source: PyTA project
    blog post raineyang.hashnode.dev)
  COMMON PATTERN: `if condition: return value` without `else` branch — the else
    falls through implicitly.
  FIX: Add explicit else branch, or ensure final elif becomes else.
  DIFFERS FROM: mypy's `return` error code (which checks type compatibility, not
    consistency). mypy requires `--warn-no-return` or checks return type.
  CLASSIFICATION: FLOW-NATIVE (requires full CFG with terminal-node analysis)
  TOOLS: pylint (R1710); mypy (--no-implicit-reexport, error code `return`);
    pyright (reportReturnType); CodeQL (py/mixed-returns)
<<<END>>>

