id: 10
ts: 2026-05-29T10:09:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: CodeQL py/mixed-returns (Explicit returns mixed with implicit fall-through returns)
authors: GitHub Security Lab
year: 2024
venue: CodeQL query help
quality: DOCS
url: https://codeql.github.com/codeql-query-help/python/py-mixed-returns/
keywords: mixed returns, implicit return, fall-through, CodeQL, CFG, inconsistent return
cites:
cited_by:
content:
  QUERY ID: py/mixed-returns
  SEVERITY: Recommendation
  PRECISION: High
  WHAT IT FLAGS: Functions containing both explicit `return value` statements AND implicit
    returns (code falling off the end without explicit return). Indicates potentially
    forgotten return statements.
  FLOW PROPERTY: Control flow analysis to identify all return paths within a function —
    distinguish between explicit `return expr` and implicit fall-through. Requires finding
    all terminal CFG nodes (those with no successor in the function) and classifying them.
  EXAMPLE:
    def check_state1(state, interactive=True):
        if not state['good'] or not state['bad']:
            if (...) and interactive:
                return  # implicit None
            # ... raises
        return True  # explicit value — inconsistent!
  CLASSIFICATION: FLOW-NATIVE (CFG terminal-node analysis)
  TOOLS: CodeQL (py/mixed-returns); pylint (R1710); mypy (return error code)
<<<END>>>

SOURCE
id: 11
ts: 2026-05-29T10:10:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: pyflakes/pylint/ruff F841/W0612 Unused Variable / Dead Store
authors: PyCQA, pylint-dev, astral-sh
year: 2024
venue: multiple
quality: DOCS
url: https://docs.astral.sh/ruff/rules/unused-variable/
keywords: unused variable, dead store, F841, W0612, liveness, def-use
cites:
cited_by:
content:
  RULE IDs: F841 (pyflakes/ruff), W0612 (pylint unused-variable)
  WHAT THEY FLAG: Variables that are assigned within functions but never subsequently
    read. The assignment exists but has no downstream use — a "dead store."
  FLOW PROPERTY: Liveness analysis / def-use chains. A variable assignment is "dead"
    if there is no reaching USE of that name after the assignment on any execution path.
    Full implementation requires: (1) computing all uses of a name after an assignment
    point, (2) checking whether any use is reachable from the assignment via CFG.
    NOTE: pyflakes and pylint do NOT implement full liveness analysis. They do
    scope-level tracking: if a name is NEVER referenced at all in its scope, it is
    flagged. They do NOT catch the "final assignment never read" (dead store) case
    where a variable IS read earlier but the last assignment before a return is unused.
    That case is tracked in pylint issue #5838 ("Lint unused variable assignment /
    dead store") as a known gap.
  RUFF F841 SPECIFICS: "Checks for the presence of unused variables in function scopes."
    Respects `lint.dummy-variable-rgx` — variables prefixed with _ are excluded.
    Fix is UNSAFE (removing assignments could delete comments). Excludes unpacked
    assignments (x, y = 1, 2) — see unused-unpacked-variable rule.
  PYLINT W0612 SPECIFICS: "Used when a variable is defined but not used." Created by
    variables checker. Skipped if a variable named with _ prefix. NOTE: "This message
    does not appear if there was any usage of the variable in the past" — confirms
    it is NOT a true dead-store detector.
  VULTURE: Detects dead stores via AST name tracking (not full liveness). Confidence
    60% for attributes/functions/variables, 100% for unreachable code.
  FALSE POSITIVES: Dynamic access (getattr, locals()), pytest fixtures, __all__,
    intentionally ignored return values.
  CLASSIFICATION: AST-LINT for the "never referenced at all" case. FLOW-NATIVE only
    if true dead-store (last assignment before exit not read) is required.
  TOOLS: ruff (F841), pyflakes (F841), pylint (W0612)
<<<END>>>

