    (issue #1880). Dynamic access patterns.
  CLASSIFICATION: AST-LINT
  TOOLS: pylint (W0612, W0613), ruff (F841 for variables, ARG001-ARG006 for arguments)
<<<END>>>

SOURCE
id: 14
ts: 2026-05-29T10:13:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: pylint W0702 bare-except / W0703 broad-except (Exception Swallowing)
authors: pylint-dev
year: 2024
venue: pylint readthedocs
quality: DOCS
url: https://pylint.pycqa.org/en/stable/user_guide/messages/warning/bare-except.html
keywords: bare-except, broad-except, exception swallowing, W0702, W0703
cites:
cited_by:
content:
  RULE IDs: W0702 (bare-except), W0703 (broad-except), W0718 (broad-exception-caught,
    newer rename of W0703 in recent pylint)
  W0702 BARE-EXCEPT: "A bare except: clause will catch SystemExit and KeyboardInterrupt
    exceptions, making it harder to interrupt a program with Control-C, and can disguise
    other problems. If you want to catch all exceptions that signal program errors, use
    except Exception: (bare except is equivalent to except BaseException:)"
  W0703 BROAD-EXCEPT: "If you use a naked except Exception: clause, you might end up
    catching exceptions other than the ones you expect, hiding bugs or making it harder
    to debug."
  EXCEPTION SWALLOWING PATTERN: `except: pass` or `except Exception: pass` — exception
    is caught and discarded without re-raise, logging, or other handling. pylint's W0702/
    W0703 flag broad catching but do NOT specifically check whether the exception is
    re-raised or logged. That "exception is swallowed entirely (pass or no re-raise)"
    check is a separate concern.
  CONTROL-FLOW ASPECT: "Relax W0702 so that it does not trigger if the exception is
    immediately re-raised." A properly implemented swallow-detector needs: walk the
    except-clause body; check if ANY path within it executes a bare `raise` or
    `raise e`; if not, the exception is swallowed. This IS a flow property.
  CLASSIFICATION: W0702/W0703 alone: AST-LINT (pattern: is the except clause bare/broad?).
    Full swallow detection (no re-raise on any path): FLOW-NATIVE (CFG of except body).
  TOOLS: pylint (W0702, W0703), ruff (E722 bare-except, BLE001 broad-exception-caught),
    flake8-bugbear (B001 do not use bare except, B002),
    bandit (B110 try-except-pass)
<<<END>>>

SOURCE
id: 15
ts: 2026-05-29T10:14:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: pylint R1732 consider-using-with (Resource Leak)
authors: pylint-dev
year: 2024
venue: pylint readthedocs
quality: DOCS
url: https://pylint.pycqa.org/en/latest/user_guide/messages/refactor/consider-using-with.html
keywords: resource leak, consider-using-with, R1732, open file, context manager, post-dominance
cites:
cited_by:
content:
  RULE ID: R1732 (consider-using-with)
  WHAT IT FLAGS: Resource-allocating assignments or calls that could be replaced with a
    `with` block. Detects: file = open(...) followed by file.read() + file.close();
    also flags open(...).read() (immediate use without with).
  EXAMPLES:
    BAD:  file = open("apple.txt", "r", encoding="utf8"); contents = file.read(); file.close()
    BAD:  contents = open("apple.txt", "r", encoding="utf8").read()
    GOOD: with open("apple.txt", "r", encoding="utf8") as file: contents = file.read()
  FLOW PROPERTY: This is a SHALLOW heuristic in pylint's implementation. It looks for
    resource-allocating calls not already inside a with-statement. It does NOT implement
    full post-dominance analysis ("does every path from the acquire() operation reach
    a close()/release()?"). A proper resource-leak detector using post-dominators would:
    (1) identify acquire op (open(), lock.acquire(), etc.),
    (2) identify release op (close(), lock.release(), __exit__),
    (3) check that the release POST-DOMINATES the acquire in the CFG
        (i.e., every path from acquire to function-exit passes through release).
    Pylint's R1732 is conservative: it flags if you're NOT using with at all.
  FALSE POSITIVES DOCUMENTED:
    - Resources allocated in __enter__ method of a class (issue #4430)
    - Class member variables using RAII pattern (issue #4808, #7658)
    - Results returned from functions (allowed by checker)
    - Call results used in existing with-statements (allowed)
  CLASSIFICATION: AST-LINT (pattern matching for with-usage absence). A full
    post-dominance resource-leak detector would be FLOW-NATIVE.
  TOOLS: pylint (R1732); bandit (B403, B602 etc for subprocess);
    CodeQL (py/context-manager, various resource leak queries);
    semgrep (at-exit taint sinks for resource leak)
<<<END>>>

