title: flake8-bugbear B-rules (mutable defaults, loop closure, finally)
authors: PyCQA
year: 2024
venue: PyCQA/flake8-bugbear GitHub
quality: CODE
url: https://github.com/PyCQA/flake8-bugbear/blob/main/README.rst
keywords: mutable default argument, loop variable capture, finally break, bugbear, B006, B007, B012, B023
cites:
cited_by:
content:
  TOOL: flake8-bugbear (PyCQA plugin for flake8, rules also re-implemented in ruff B-rules)
  FLOW-RELEVANT RULES:
    B006 "Do not use mutable data structures for argument defaults. They are created
      during function definition time. All calls to the function reuse this one instance
      of that data structure, persisting changes between them."
      PATTERN: `def f(x=[]):` — CLASSIFICATION: AST-LINT (syntactic pattern in arg defaults)
    B007 "Loop control variable not used within the loop body. If this is intended, start
      the name with an underscore."
      PATTERN: for-loop variable never referenced inside loop.
      CLASSIFICATION: AST-LINT (similar to py/unused-loop-variable CodeQL query)
    B010 "Do not call setattr(x, 'attr', val), instead use normal property access."
      CLASSIFICATION: AST-LINT
    B012 "Use of break, continue or return inside finally blocks will silence exceptions
      or override return values from the try or except blocks. To silence an exception,
      do it explicitly in the except block."
      PATTERN: break/continue/return inside finally — these control flow escapes
      swallow any in-flight exception.
      FLOW PROPERTY: Detects whether a finally block contains a control-flow escape.
      CLASSIFICATION: AST-LINT (syntactic: is break/continue/return present in finally?)
      but the EFFECT is a flow property (exception swallowing via control-flow escape).
    B016 "Cannot raise a literal. Did you intend to return it or raise an Exception?"
      CLASSIFICATION: AST-LINT
    B017 "assertRaises(Exception) should be considered evil. Tests may pass even if
      the code being tested is never executed due to a typo."
      CLASSIFICATION: AST-LINT
    B023 "Functions defined inside a loop must not use variables redefined in the loop,
      because late-binding closures are a classic gotcha."
      PATTERN: lambda or nested function inside for/while that captures the loop variable.
      FLOW PROPERTY: Requires knowing that: (1) function is defined inside loop,
      (2) the function body uses a name that is also the loop variable.
      CLASSIFICATION: AST-LINT (syntactic scope analysis) — simpler than CodeQL
      py/loop-variable-capture which also checks call-after-loop.
    B024 "Abstract base class has methods, but none of them are abstract."
      CLASSIFICATION: AST-LINT
    B026 "Star-arg unpacking after keyword argument."
      CLASSIFICATION: AST-LINT
  TOOLS: flake8-bugbear (original), ruff (re-implements all B-rules natively)
<<<END>>>

