id: 19
ts: 2026-05-29T10:18:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: CodeQL py/loop-variable-capture
authors: GitHub Security Lab
year: 2024
venue: CodeQL query help
quality: DOCS
url: https://codeql.github.com/codeql-query-help/python/py-loop-variable-capture/
keywords: loop variable capture, late-binding closure, lambda, for loop, CodeQL
cites:
cited_by:
content:
  QUERY ID: py/loop-variable-capture
  SEVERITY: Error
  PRECISION: High
  WHAT IT FLAGS: Nested functions or lambda expressions that capture loop variables
    (late-binding closures). When the closure is called AFTER the loop ends, it uses
    the value from the LAST loop iteration, not the iteration where it was created.
  TECHNICAL DETAIL: "In Python, a nested function or lambda expression that captures
    a variable from its surrounding scope is a late-binding closure, meaning that the
    value of the variable is determined when the closure is called, not when it is
    created. If the closure is called after the loop ends, it will use the value of the
    variable on the last iteration."
  EXAMPLE:
    tasks = []
    for i in range(5):
        tasks.append(lambda: print(i))
    for t in tasks:
        t()  # Prints 4,4,4,4,4 NOT 0,1,2,3,4
  FIXES:
    Use default parameter: lambda i=i: print(i)
    Use functools.partial
  FLOW PROPERTY: Requires scope analysis to identify: (1) function/lambda defined inside
    loop, (2) loop variable referenced in closure body, (3) closure invoked AFTER loop.
    The third condition requires interprocedural or use-site analysis.
  CLASSIFICATION: FLOW-NATIVE (scope + call-site analysis) for full detection.
    AST-LINT if only checking syntactic capture (not verifying post-loop call).
  TOOLS: CodeQL (py/loop-variable-capture); flake8-bugbear (B023, syntactic only);
    ruff (B023 re-implementation)
<<<END>>>

