id: 21
ts: 2026-05-29T10:20:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: CodeQL py/uninitialized-local-variable (Potentially Uninitialized)
authors: GitHub Security Lab
year: 2024
venue: CodeQL query help
quality: DOCS
url: https://codeql.github.com/codeql-query-help/python/py-uninitialized-local-variable/
keywords: uninitialized local variable, use-before-def, CodeQL, UnboundLocalError
cites:
cited_by:
content:
  QUERY ID: py/uninitialized-local-variable (NOTE: searches also return py/possibly-unbound-local
    as same concept — query name may vary by CodeQL version)
  SEVERITY: Error
  PRECISION: Medium
  WHAT IT FLAGS: Local variables used before assignment in functions. When a variable is
    assigned anywhere in a function without explicit global/nonlocal declaration, Python
    treats it as local. Using it before initialization triggers UnboundLocalError.
  KEY DESCRIPTION: "If a variable is assigned to in a function and not explicitly declared
    global or nonlocal, then it is assumed to be a local variable. If it is used before
    it is defined, then an UnboundLocalError will be raised."
  EXAMPLE (problematic):
    def test1():
        var = 2
        def print_var():
            print var      # Error: used before var = 3 in same scope
            var = 3
  FIXES: Add global/nonlocal declaration, or initialize before use.
  FLOW PROPERTY: CFG-based reaching definitions. The query checks that every USE of
    a local variable has a reaching DEFINITION on all paths from function entry.
  CLASSIFICATION: FLOW-NATIVE (requires reaching definitions analysis)
  TOOLS: CodeQL (py/uninitialized-local-variable); pylint (E0601 used-before-assignment);
    pyflakes (F823, linear-case only); pyright (reportUnboundVariable)
<<<END>>>

