id: 24
ts: 2026-05-29T10:23:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: vulture — Find Dead Python Code
authors: Jendrik Seipp
year: 2024
venue: jendrikseipp/vulture GitHub
quality: CODE
url: https://github.com/jendrikseipp/vulture
keywords: dead code, unused functions, unused classes, unreachable code, vulture, confidence
cites:
cited_by:
content:
  TOOL: vulture
  WHAT IT DETECTS: Unused functions, classes, methods, properties, imports, variables.
    Unreachable code after return/break/continue/raise. "Unsatisfiable conditional
    statements" (always-true/false conditions).
  IMPLEMENTATION: Uses Python's ast module to build ASTs. Traverses all syntax trees
    recording names of DEFINED and USED objects. Reports defined-but-never-used names.
    "Ignores scopes and only takes object names into account" — does not distinguish
    local vs global namespace.
  CONFIDENCE SCORING:
    100% — function/method/class arguments, unreachable code (guaranteed dead)
    90%  — imports
    60%  — attributes, classes, functions, methods, properties, variables
    Values below 100% are "very rough estimates based on type of code chunk"
  FLAG: --min-confidence 100 to only report guaranteed dead code
  WHITELIST MECHANISM: Create Python file simulating usage of "dead" code.
    `vulture --make-whitelist > whitelist.py` to auto-generate.
  FALSE POSITIVES: Dynamic attribute injection, getattr, eval, exec, __all__,
    callback registrations, decorators, test fixtures.
    Use --ignore-names, --ignore-decorators, underscore-prefix, or whitelist.
  DIFFERS FROM: pyflakes/pylint — those check per-scope; vulture checks across
    whole codebase (finds unused public APIs, not just local vars).
  CLASSIFICATION: AST-LINT (name presence tracking). Not full liveness analysis.
    Unreachable code detection is basic (post-return/raise in same block).
  TOOLS: vulture (standalone)
<<<END>>>

