id: 26
ts: 2026-05-29T10:25:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: Semgrep Dataflow Analysis Engine
authors: Semgrep (r2c)
year: 2024
venue: semgrep.dev docs
quality: DOCS
url: https://semgrep.dev/docs/writing-rules/data-flow/data-flow-overview
keywords: semgrep, taint mode, dataflow, intra-procedural, resource leak, at-exit
cites:
cited_by:
content:
  TOOL: Semgrep (Community Edition + Pro Engine)
  DATAFLOW ENGINE: Intra-procedural dataflow analysis (CE). Inter-file/interprocedural
    (Pro Engine only — tracks taint across functions within one file, but does NOT
    cross file boundaries).
  TAINT MODE: mode: taint in rule YAML. Reports when tainted data (from sources) reaches
    a vulnerable function (sink) without being sanitized. Sources → propagators
    (assignments, function calls) → sinks.
  RESOURCE LEAK VIA AT-EXIT SINKS (Pro): "at-exit: true restricts a sink specification
    to only match at exit statements, or statements after which the control-flow will
    exit the function being analyzed." This enables leak detection rules: if a resource
    (e.g., open file handle) reaches a function exit without being closed, flag it.
    This IS a post-dominance property: release must post-dominate acquire.
  CONSTANT PROPAGATION: Semgrep CE includes constant propagation in its dataflow engine,
    enabling detection of constant conditions.
  PYTHON COVERAGE: Framework-specific Pro rules require Pro Engine; CE has basic
    dataflow for Python.
  FALSE POSITIVES: Conservative: when taint flows into functions without source,
    may assume propagation. at-exit false positives from complex control flow.
  CLASSIFICATION: FLOW-NATIVE (taint/dataflow); at-exit-sinks for resource leaks
    require post-dominance reasoning.
  TOOLS: Semgrep (CE for basic rules; Pro Engine for interprocedural + at-exit)
<<<END>>>


