id: 27
ts: 2026-05-29T10:30:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: CodeQL py/constant-conditional-expression and py/comparison-of-constants (Always-True/False Conditions)
authors: GitHub Security Lab
year: 2024
venue: CodeQL query help
quality: DOCS
url: https://codeql.github.com/codeql-query-help/python/py-constant-conditional-expression/
keywords: constant condition, always-true, always-false, dead code, tautology, CWE-570, CWE-571
cites:
cited_by:
content:
  QUERY IDs:
    py/constant-conditional-expression — constant value as condition in if/while
    py/comparison-of-constants — two constants compared to each other
    py/redundant-comparison — comparison outcome can be inferred from context/prior comparisons
  SEVERITIES: Warning (all three)
  PRECISIONS: py/comparison-of-constants: very-high; py/redundant-comparison: high
  CWEs: CWE-561 (dead code), CWE-570 (always false), CWE-571 (always true)
  WHAT THEY FLAG:
    py/constant-conditional-expression: `if True:`, `if 1 > 100:`, `while False:` —
      conditional is a literal constant, only one branch ever runs. Dead code.
    py/comparison-of-constants: `foo = 1 == 1` — both sides of comparison are constants.
      "The Boolean value of the comparison will always be the same."
    py/redundant-comparison: same comparison appears in both if/elif branches; the elif
      comparison is always False given the if already checked it.
      EXAMPLE: if obj1 < obj2: return -1; elif obj1 < obj2: return 1  # elif unreachable
  FLOW PROPERTY:
    py/constant-conditional-expression: constant value analysis only (no CFG needed for
      literal constants; type-narrowing needed for more complex always-true/false via types)
    py/redundant-comparison: requires CFG-level reasoning — knowing that execution reaching
      the elif implies the if-condition was false, making the same comparison redundant.
  RUFF EQUIVALENT: PLR0133 (comparison-of-constant) — "Comparing two constants will always
    resolve to the same value, so the comparison is redundant." Added in ruff v0.0.221.
  PYLINT EQUIVALENT: R0133 (comparison-of-constants) in pylint.
  MYPY EQUIVALENT: `redundant-expr` error code — "Mypy generates an error if it thinks
    that an expression is redundant." Triggered by type narrowing making conditions always
    true/false.
  CLASSIFICATION:
    py/comparison-of-constants: AST-LINT (syntactic pattern)
    py/redundant-comparison: FLOW-NATIVE (requires CFG to know prior comparison result)
    mypy redundant-expr: FLOW-NATIVE (type narrowing + control flow)
  TOOLS: CodeQL (all three), ruff (PLR0133), pylint (R0133), mypy (redundant-expr)
<<<END>>>

SOURCE
id: 28
ts: 2026-05-29T10:31:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: Unreachable Code Detection via Type Narrowing — Never/NoReturn, assert_never, exhaustiveness
authors: python/typing, mypy, pyright
year: 2024
venue: typing.python.org
quality: DOCS
url: https://typing.python.org/en/latest/guides/unreachable.html
keywords: Never, NoReturn, unreachable, exhaustiveness, assert_never, type narrowing, dead branch
cites:
cited_by:
content:
  MECHANISM: Type checkers detect unreachable code via the bottom type (Never/NoReturn).
    A code path is flagged as unreachable when the type checker can prove the type at
    that point is Never — meaning no value can reach that code.
  NEVER vs NORE_RETURN: Python 3.11 added Never as explicit bottom type. Never and
    NoReturn are identical to type checkers. Both represent "this function never returns"
    or "this code is unreachable."
  EXHAUSTIVENESS CHECKING PATTERN:
    ```python
    def assert_never(arg: Never) -> Never:
        raise AssertionError("Expected code to be unreachable")
    ```
    Placing assert_never() in default/catch-all branches forces type checker to verify
    all cases are handled. If new enum member added without updating match statement,
    type checker flags error — new variant would reach assert_never(), violating Never contract.
  PYRIGHT:
    - reportMatchNotExhaustive: enabled by default in strict mode. Direct exhaustiveness
      checking without requiring assert_never().
    - reportUnreachable: flags code determined to be structurally or type-analysis unreachable.
      Default: "none" (but basedpyright makes it default: "warning").
      NOTE: Pyright's version does NOT report sys.version_info or sys.platform checks as
      unreachable (common false-negative).
  MYPY:
    - --warn-unreachable: generates error for unreachable statements (type-analysis-based)
    - exhaustive-match: enables exhaustiveness checking via error code
    - redundant-expr: flags redundant expressions determined always true/false
  BASEDPYRIGHT IMPROVEMENTS:
    - reportUnreachable is the first new rule added to basedpyright
    - Reports unreachable code that standard pyright misses (sys.version_info checks)
    - Unreachable code is NOT type-checked in pyright — basedpyright fixes this
    - Introduces "hint" diagnostic category for unreachable code hints
  CLASSIFICATION: FLOW-NATIVE (type narrowing + type-state propagation through CFG)
  TOOLS: pyright (reportUnreachable, reportMatchNotExhaustive), mypy (--warn-unreachable,
    redundant-expr, exhaustive-match), basedpyright (improved reportUnreachable)
<<<END>>>

