id: 33
ts: 2026-05-29T10:36:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: Beniget — Use-Def Chains for Python Programs
authors: Serge Guelton (pythran/serge-sans-paille)
year: 2020
venue: pythran-stories blog / PyPI
quality: BLOG
url: https://serge-sans-paille.github.io/pythran-stories/gast-beniget-use-def-chains-for-python-programs.html
keywords: use-def chains, def-use, beniget, gast, dead code, unused imports, constant folding
cites:
cited_by:
content:
  TOOL: beniget (Python package, BSD 3-clause, supports Python 2.7 and 3.6+)
  WHAT IT PROVIDES: Use-def chains — compiler abstraction linking each USE of an
    identifier to its DEFINITION(s). Enables: unused import detection, closure computation,
    NameError detection (uses without definitions), constant folding support.
  IMPLEMENTATION: Works on gast (Generic AST) — unified AST structure across Python
    versions. Traverses AST recording DEF and USE events. Builds explicit def→use and
    use→def edges.
  EXAMPLE:
    a = 1        # DEF(a)
    b = a + 2    # DEF(b) USE(a)
    print(a, b)  # USE(a, b)
  DEAD STORE DETECTION: Possible by finding DEFs with no downstream USEs in the chain.
    This IS a true def-use chain approach and CAN detect last-write-never-read.
  LIMITATIONS: "Cannot reliably handle Python's dynamic features including eval and exec,
    star imports, and assigning to globals() or locals()."
    "Performs non-data-dependent analysis, meaning conditional assignments don't guarantee
    definitions." — i.e., no path-sensitivity.
  CONTRAST WITH PYFLAKES/PYLINT: Both pyflakes and pylint do scope-level name tracking
    (name exists/doesn't in scope). Beniget builds actual edges between definition sites
    and use sites, enabling dead-store analysis that pyflakes/pylint miss.
  AVAILABILITY: pip install beniget; GitHub: github.com/serge-sans-paille/beniget
  CLASSIFICATION: FLOW-NATIVE (def-use chain construction)
  NOTE: This is a LIBRARY for analysis, not a standalone checker.
  TOOLS: beniget (library); pythran compiler uses it
<<<END>>>

