id: 36
ts: 2026-05-29T10:39:00Z
relevance: MED
title: pylint W0104 pointless-statement / W0106 expression-not-assigned
authors: pylint-dev
year: 2024
venue: pylint readthedocs
quality: DOCS
url: https://pylint.readthedocs.io/en/stable/user_guide/messages/warning/pointless-statement.html
keywords: pointless statement, expression not assigned, W0104, W0106, dead expression
cites:
cited_by:
content:
  RULE IDs: W0104 (pointless-statement), W0106 (expression-not-assigned)
  W0104: "Used when a statement doesn't have (or at least seems to) any effect."
    Fires on bare expressions in function bodies: `x + 1` (result discarded), `"docstring
    not at start"` (looks like misplaced docstring).
  W0106: "Used when an expression that is not a function call is assigned to nothing."
    Related to expression results being discarded.
  CLASSIFICATION: AST-LINT (syntactic: statement is bare expression with no assignment
    and no call effect). NOT a dataflow check — doesn't verify whether the expression
    has side effects.
  FALSE POSITIVES: Custom objects where __add__ has side effects, intentional value
    computation for side effects (uncommon but valid Python).
  NOTE: These are LIGHTER-WEIGHT than dead-store detectors. Dead stores (assignment
    whose value is never used) are more useful; these catch entirely discarded expression
    results.
  TOOLS: pylint (W0104, W0106); ruff has related checks in B/PIE categories
<<<END>>>

SOURCE
id: 37
ts: 2026-05-29T10:40:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: Resource Leak — Post-Dominance Property Formal Definition for acquire/release
authors: (synthesis from Semgrep, CodeQL, Pysa docs)
year: 2024
venue: synthesis record
quality: DOCS
url: https://semgrep.dev/docs/writing-rules/data-flow/taint-mode/overview
keywords: resource leak, post-dominance, acquire, release, must-reach, file handle, lock
cites:
cited_by:
content:
  FORMAL PROPERTY: A resource leak occurs when acquire(r) is not post-dominated by
    release(r) in the CFG. Formally: for every path from acquire(r) to function exit,
    that path must pass through release(r). If any path from acquire to exit DOES NOT
    pass through release, there is a potential leak.
  EQUIVALENTLY (in terms of post-dominators): release must be in the post-dominator
    set of acquire — every path from acquire to the exit passes through release.
  MUST-REACH vs MAY-REACH: We need release to MUST-reach from acquire on ALL paths
    (post-dominance). If release only MAY-reach (on some paths), there is a conditional
    leak (resource leaked on exception paths).
  PYTHON CONTEXT: Python's `with` statement (context managers) is the canonical solution.
    The __exit__ method is guaranteed to be called even on exception paths. Static
    detection checks whether resources that implement __enter__/__exit__ are used
    with `with` or manually managed.
  IMPLEMENTATION APPROACHES:
    1. pylint R1732 (consider-using-with): SHALLOW — checks if resource NOT inside with.
       Does not do post-dominance analysis. Does not track whether close() is called
       on all paths.
    2. semgrep at-exit taint sinks: PRO feature. `at-exit: true` restricts sink to
       match only at function exit statements. Enables: if open() handle taint reaches
       exit without close(), flag it. This IS post-dominance reasoning.
    3. CodeQL: Has context-manager and resource-tracking queries. Uses CFG-level
       analysis. Full post-dominance possible.
    4. Pysa: Frame security as taint — file handle "tainted" by open(), "sanitized"
       by close(). If taint reaches function exit (at-exit sink), flag.
  LOCK ACQUIRE/RELEASE: Same post-dominance property. lock.acquire() should be
    post-dominated by lock.release(). Python's threading.Lock used as context manager
    (`with lock:`) guarantees this.
  FALSE POSITIVES: RAII patterns, transfers of ownership, resources stored in class
    attributes for later release.
  CLASSIFICATION: FLOW-NATIVE (requires CFG post-dominance analysis or must-reach)
  TOOLS: pylint (R1732, shallow), semgrep (at-exit, Pro), CodeQL (full CFG),
    pysa/pyre (taint-based)
<<<END>>>

SOURCE
id: 38
ts: 2026-05-29T10:41:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: CodeQL Python CFG Analysis — ControlFlowNode, AstNode, BasicBlock
authors: GitHub / CodeQL team
year: 2024
venue: CodeQL docs
quality: DOCS
url: https://codeql.github.com/docs/codeql-language-guides/analyzing-control-flow-in-python/
keywords: CodeQL CFG, ControlFlowNode, AstNode, BasicBlock, unreachable, Python CFG
cites:
cited_by:
content:
  CORE DESIGN: One-to-many: each AstNode maps to zero, one, or MANY ControlFlowNodes.
    Each ControlFlowNode maps to exactly ONE AstNode.
  WHY MANY: Multiple execution paths can flow through a single code statement. Example:
    try-finally block with conditional breaks — close_resource() has three distinct
    control flow paths: normal execution, exception handling, and loop breaks.
  UNREACHABLE DETECTION QUERY:
    "Any AstNode that is unreachable has no paths flowing through it. Therefore, any
    AstNode without a corresponding ControlFlowNode is unreachable."
    Query should target statement nodes specifically (not all AstNodes) — modules
    have no source code representation, causing false positives if not filtered.
  BASICBLOCK ANALYSIS:
    BasicBlocks aggregate ControlFlowNodes into larger units with similar reachability.
    Reduces computational overhead — fewer blocks than individual nodes.
    Key predicate: strictlyReaches() — checks inter-block reachability.
    Use: identify mutually exclusive code blocks by checking whether basic blocks can
    reach each other via strictlyReaches().
  DOMINATOR ANALYSIS: CodeQL provides dominator tree analysis for Python CFGs,
    enabling post-dominance queries for resource-leak detection.
  CLASSIFICATION: FLOW-NATIVE (full CFG with dominators)
  TOOLS: CodeQL (Python CFG library — codeql/python)
<<<END>>>


