id: 40
ts: 2026-05-29T10:51:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: pylint W2901 / ruff PLW2901 redefined-loop-name (Loop Variable Reassignment)
authors: pylint-dev, astral-sh
year: 2024
venue: pylint readthedocs, ruff docs
quality: DOCS
url: https://docs.astral.sh/ruff/rules/redefined-loop-name/
keywords: redefined-loop-name, loop variable reassignment, PLW2901, W2901
cites:
cited_by:
content:
  RULE IDs: W2901 (pylint redefined-loop-name), PLW2901 (ruff re-implementation)
  WHAT IT FLAGS: "Redefinition of a loop variable inside the loop's body causes its
    value to differ from the original loop iteration for the remainder of the block,
    in a way that will likely cause bugs."
  BEHAVIOR: In Python, for loops and with statements don't define their own scopes.
    A nested loop using the same target variable as an outer loop reuses the same
    variable; the inner loop's final value "leaks out" into the remainder of the outer loop.
  EXAMPLE: for x in items: x = x.strip()  # x is now stripped value, not original item
  FALSE POSITIVE: Augmented assignment (x += 1) is sometimes incorrectly flagged —
    in-place augmented assignment (PLW2901 issue #7273). The rule fires on any
    reassignment to the loop variable, including intentional transformations.
  CLASSIFICATION: AST-LINT (syntactic: assignment to loop variable within loop body).
    No CFG needed — pure AST pattern matching.
  TOOLS: pylint (W2901), ruff (PLW2901)
<<<END>>>

