id: 41
ts: 2026-05-29T10:52:00Z
relevance: MED
title: ruff RET504/RET505/RET506 — Unnecessary Variable Assignment Before Return
authors: astral-sh
year: 2024
venue: ruff docs
quality: DOCS
url: https://docs.astral.sh/ruff/rules/unnecessary-assign/
keywords: unnecessary-assign, dead store, RET504, RET505, RET506, return optimization
cites:
cited_by:
content:
  RULE IDs:
    RET504 (unnecessary-assign): "Variable assignment immediately before return of
      that variable is unnecessary. The variable assignment is not necessary, as the
      value can be returned directly."
    RET505 (superfluous-else-return): Checks for else statements with a return in
      the preceding if block. The else is redundant since the if already returns.
    RET506 (superfluous-else-raise): Same as RET505 but for raise in preceding if.
  FLOW PROPERTY: RET504 is a LIMITED dead-store check: it only catches the specific
    pattern of `x = expr; return x` — the assignment is unnecessary because x is
    immediately returned. This is simpler than full liveness analysis (which would
    catch any dead store regardless of what follows).
  RET505/RET506 FLOW PROPERTY: Requires knowing that the if block always exits (via
    return/raise), making the else branch's execution condition unconditional. This
    IS a CFG property: if the if-block always terminates, the else is effectively a
    new block with no special condition.
  KNOWN ISSUES:
    - RET504 false positive when variable is built up incrementally before return
      (issue #2950).
    - RET505/RET506 don't work for else after return/raise inside try/except (issue #9625).
  CLASSIFICATION:
    RET504: FLOW-NATIVE (limited dead-store check, but shallow — only immediate return)
    RET505/RET506: FLOW-NATIVE (requires knowing prior if-block terminates)
  TOOLS: ruff (RET504, RET505, RET506); pylint (R1705 no-else-return)
<<<END>>>

