id: 42
ts: 2026-05-29T10:53:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: pyright reportUninitializedInstanceVariable — Uninitialized Attribute Detection
authors: Microsoft
year: 2024
venue: pyright GitHub issues
quality: DOCS
url: https://github.com/microsoft/pyright/issues/3431
keywords: reportUninitializedInstanceVariable, instance attribute, uninitialized, dataflow, pyright
cites:
cited_by:
content:
  RULE ID: reportUninitializedInstanceVariable (pyright)
  WHAT IT FLAGS: Instance attributes that are declared (type-annotated) in the class
    body but not initialized in __init__ or any always-executing code path.
  FLOW PROPERTY: Requires checking: (1) all annotations in class body, (2) whether
    each annotated attribute is assigned in __init__ on ALL control flow paths, or
    in __init__'s callees, or via class-level default.
  FALSE POSITIVES (EXTENSIVE):
    - Dataclasses: attributes initialized by @dataclass generated __init__ — pyright
      sometimes doesn't recognize (issue #3431, #9623).
    - ABC subclasses: abstract class attributes not initialized in abstract base (issue
      #5103, #8205).
    - @dataclass field(init=False): Pyright does not check that field is initialized in
      __post_init__ (issue #4701).
    - unittest setUp methods: attributes initialized in setUp not recognized (discussion
      #4834).
    - NamedTuple: false positives on v1.1.368+ (issue #8184).
    - Constructor-called reset() methods: pyright doesn't track through helper methods.
  BASEDPYRIGHT: Provides "improved logic for detecting uninitialized instance variables"
    as one of its benefits over standard pyright.
  CLASSIFICATION: FLOW-NATIVE (requires intra-procedural dataflow within __init__ to
    determine if all paths assign the attribute)
  TOOLS: pyright (reportUninitializedInstanceVariable), basedpyright (improved version)
<<<END>>>

