  RULE ID: B018 (useless-expression), B020 (loop-variable-overrides-iterator)
  B018 WHAT IT FLAGS: "Found useless expression. Either assign it to a variable or
    remove it." Standalone expressions whose results are not used. Equivalent to
    pylint W0104 (pointless-statement).
    FALSE POSITIVES: Attribute access with side effects (issue #10536), descriptor
    accesses (issue #9082).
  B020 WHAT IT FLAGS: "Loop control variable {name} overrides the loop iterable."
    `for items in items:` — the loop variable shadows the iterable being iterated.
    This can lead to confusing behavior or bugs after the loop.
  CLASSIFICATION: Both AST-LINT
  TOOLS: ruff (B018, B020)
<<<END>>>


SOURCE
id: 50
ts: 2026-05-29T11:10:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: MASTER CLASSIFICATION TABLE — All Detectors by Flow-Native vs AST-Lint
authors: (synthesis record — all sources above)
year: 2026
venue: synthesis
quality: DOCS
url: (synthesis)
keywords: classification, flow-native, AST-lint, ranking, CFG, dataflow, detectors
cites: all preceding records
cited_by:
content:
  CLASSIFICATION CRITERIA:
    FLOW-NATIVE: Correctness requires a CFG, reaching definitions, dominators,
      post-dominators, liveness, or type-state lattice.
    AST-LINT: Detectable via AST pattern matching or simple scope-level name tracking;
      no control-flow traversal required for correct detection.
    Note: "Syntactic + Flow Effect" = syntactically simple to DETECT but represents a FLOW PROPERTY.

  ============================================================
  FLOW-NATIVE DETECTORS
  ============================================================

  1. USE-BEFORE-DEFINITION (possibly-unbound, branching case)
     FLOW PROPERTY: Reaching definitions / dominator analysis. Every path from
       function entry to USE must pass through a DEF.
     TOOLS: pyright (reportPossiblyUnbound, reportUnboundVariable), mypy (possibly-undefined),
       pylint (E0601 used-before-assignment, E0606 possibly-used-before-assignment),
       CodeQL (py/uninitialized-local-variable)
     RULE IDs: E0601, E0606 (pylint); reportUnboundVariable, reportPossiblyUnbound (pyright);
       possibly-undefined (mypy); py/uninitialized-local-variable (CodeQL); F823 (pyflakes, linear-only)
     FALSE POSITIVE RATE: MED (try/except/finally cases; exhaustive elif chains; loops)
     REVIEW VALUE: VERY HIGH (definite runtime error)
     RANK: #1

  2. UNREACHABLE CODE (post-terminator; type-narrowing-based)
     FLOW PROPERTY: CFG reachability — nodes with no incoming edges from entry.
     TOOLS: CodeQL (py/unreachable-statement), pylint (W0101, shallow),
       pyright (reportUnreachable), mypy (--warn-unreachable), vulture
     RULE IDs: W0101 (pylint); py/unreachable-statement (CodeQL);
       reportUnreachable (pyright); unreachable (mypy)
     FALSE POSITIVE RATE: LOW for post-return/raise; MED for type-narrowing-based
     REVIEW VALUE: HIGH (dead code)
     RANK: #2

  3. INCONSISTENT / MISSING RETURN (not all paths return)
     FLOW PROPERTY: CFG terminal-node analysis — all exits must have explicit return.
     TOOLS: pylint (R1710), mypy (return error code), pyright (reportReturnType),
       CodeQL (py/mixed-returns)
     RULE IDs: R1710 (pylint); return (mypy); reportReturnType (pyright);
       py/mixed-returns (CodeQL)
     FALSE POSITIVE RATE: LOW (well-defined property)
     REVIEW VALUE: HIGH (implicit None returns often bugs)
     RANK: #3

  4. DEAD STORE / ASSIGNMENT NEVER READ (full liveness)
     FLOW PROPERTY: Liveness analysis — def-use chains; assignment with no downstream use.
     NOTE: Most tools only check "never referenced at all in scope" (AST-LINT).
       True dead-store (last assignment never read) is NOT implemented in pylint/pyflakes/ruff
       as of 2024 (pylint issue #5838). Beniget library provides def-use chains for this.
     TOOLS: pyflakes/ruff (F841 — partial: never referenced at all), pylint (W0612 — same),
       CodeQL (py/unused-local-variable — same), beniget (true def-use chains)
     RULE IDs: F841 (pyflakes/ruff); W0612 (pylint); py/unused-local-variable (CodeQL)
     FALSE POSITIVE RATE: LOW for "never used at all"; HIGHER for full dead-store
     REVIEW VALUE: HIGH for true dead-store; MED for "never used at all"
     RANK: #4

  5. RESOURCE LEAK (acquire not post-dominated by release)
     FLOW PROPERTY: Post-dominance — every path from acquire to function exit passes
       through release.
     TOOLS: pylint (R1732, shallow — just checks for with-usage absence),
       semgrep (at-exit sinks, Pro), CodeQL (full CFG), pysa/pyre (taint-based)
     RULE IDs: R1732 (pylint); py/context-manager* (CodeQL); at-exit taint (semgrep)
     FALSE POSITIVE RATE: MED-HIGH for shallow checks; LOW for full post-dominance
     REVIEW VALUE: HIGH (real resource leaks)
     RANK: #5

  6. NONE DEREFERENCE / OPTIONAL MISUSE
     FLOW PROPERTY: Type-state narrowing — None is narrowed away by isinstance/is None checks.
     TOOLS: pyright (reportOptionalMemberAccess, reportOptionalSubscript, etc.),
       mypy (strict-optional)
     RULE IDs: reportOptional* (pyright); [attr-defined] on Optional (mypy)
     FALSE POSITIVE RATE: LOW with good type annotations; HIGH without annotations
     REVIEW VALUE: HIGH (frequent runtime errors)
     RANK: #6

  7. LOOP VARIABLE AFTER EMPTY LOOP (possibly-unbound post-loop)
     FLOW PROPERTY: Loop-path reaching definitions — def exists only if loop body executes.
     TOOLS: pylint (W0631), pyright (reportPossiblyUnbound), mypy (possibly-undefined)
     RULE IDs: W0631 (pylint)
     FALSE POSITIVE RATE: MED (false positives when loop is guaranteed to run)
     REVIEW VALUE: HIGH (silent NameError at runtime)
     RANK: #7

  8. UNREACHABLE EXCEPT BLOCK (exception order / hierarchy)
     FLOW PROPERTY: Exception type hierarchy + exception propagation flow — superclass
       handler precedes subclass handler.
     TOOLS: CodeQL (py/unreachable-except)
     RULE IDs: py/unreachable-except (CodeQL)
     FALSE POSITIVE RATE: LOW (well-defined exception hierarchy check)
     REVIEW VALUE: HIGH (dead handler)
     RANK: #8

  9. REDUNDANT CONDITION / ALWAYS-TRUE-FALSE (via type narrowing)
     FLOW PROPERTY: Type-state propagation — after narrowing, condition is always true/false.
     TOOLS: mypy (redundant-expr), pyright (implies unreachable), ruff (PLR0133),
       CodeQL (py/redundant-comparison, py/comparison-of-constants)
     RULE IDs: redundant-expr (mypy); PLR0133 (ruff); py/redundant-comparison (CodeQL)
     FALSE POSITIVE RATE: LOW for literal constants; MED for type-narrowing-based
     REVIEW VALUE: MED (often cosmetic but catches logic errors)
     RANK: #9

  10. EXCEPTION SWALLOWING — FULL (no re-raise on any path through handler)
      FLOW PROPERTY: CFG of except handler body — does any path through handler execute
        a bare `raise`? If not, exception is swallowed.
      NOTE: Current tools only check SYNTACTIC swallowing (pass/bare-except). Full
        flow analysis needed for complex handlers.
      TOOLS: bandit (B110 — syntactic only), pylint (W0702 — syntactic), CodeQL (py/empty-except)
      RULE IDs: B110 (bandit); W0702 (pylint); py/empty-except (CodeQL)
      FALSE POSITIVE RATE: LOW for syntactic case; MED for flow-based
      REVIEW VALUE: HIGH (silent exception hiding)
      RANK: #10

  11. EXCEPTION SWALLOWING — FINALLY (return/break in finally silences exception)
      FLOW PROPERTY: CFG escape in finally block — return/break preempts exception propagation.
      TOOLS: pylint (W0150), CodeQL (py/exit-from-finally), ruff/bugbear (B012)
      RULE IDs: W0150 (pylint); py/exit-from-finally (CodeQL); B012 (bugbear/ruff)
      FALSE POSITIVE RATE: LOW (clear semantic antipattern)
      REVIEW VALUE: HIGH (silent exception loss)
      RANK: #11

  12. LOOP VARIABLE CAPTURE (late-binding closure)
      FLOW PROPERTY: Scope analysis + call-site analysis for closure-after-loop-use.
      TOOLS: CodeQL (py/loop-variable-capture), bugbear/ruff (B023, syntactic only)
      RULE IDs: py/loop-variable-capture (CodeQL); B023 (bugbear/ruff)
      FALSE POSITIVE RATE: LOW
      REVIEW VALUE: HIGH (classic Python gotcha)
      RANK: #12

  ============================================================
  AST-LINT DETECTORS (no CFG needed)
  ============================================================

  A. UNUSED IMPORT (never referenced in module)
     TOOLS: pyflakes/ruff (F401), pylint (W0611), CodeQL (py/unused-import)
     FALSE POSITIVE RATE: LOW (re-exports, side-effect imports are exceptions)
     REVIEW VALUE: MED

  B. UNUSED VARIABLE (never referenced in scope)
     TOOLS: ruff (F841, RUF059), pylint (W0612), CodeQL (py/unused-local-variable)
     NOTE: "Never referenced at all in scope" — NOT true dead-store.
     FALSE POSITIVE RATE: LOW

  C. UNUSED ARGUMENT
     TOOLS: ruff (ARG001, ARG002), pylint (W0613)
     FALSE POSITIVE RATE: MED (interface methods, pytest fixtures)

  D. MUTABLE DEFAULT ARGUMENT
     TOOLS: pylint (W0102), bugbear/ruff (B006)
     FALSE POSITIVE RATE: LOW

  E. BARE/BROAD EXCEPT (syntactic)
     TOOLS: pylint (W0702, W0703), ruff (E722, BLE001), bandit (B110)
     FALSE POSITIVE RATE: LOW (syntactic pattern)

  F. VARIABLE SHADOWING
     TOOLS: pylint (W0621 redefined-outer-name)
     FALSE POSITIVE RATE: MED (common pattern to use same name)

  G. LOOP VARIABLE UNUSED IN BODY
     TOOLS: ruff (B007, py/unused-loop-variable CodeQL)
     FALSE POSITIVE RATE: LOW

  H. COMPARISON OF CONSTANTS (two literals compared)
     TOOLS: ruff (PLR0133), CodeQL (py/comparison-of-constants)
     FALSE POSITIVE RATE: VERY LOW

  I. REDUNDANT ELSE AFTER RETURN
     TOOLS: pylint (R1705), ruff (RET505/RET506)
     FALSE POSITIVE RATE: LOW-MED (try/except edge cases)

  J. LOOP VARIABLE OVERRIDES ITERATOR
     TOOLS: ruff (B020)
     FALSE POSITIVE RATE: LOW

  K. RETURN/BREAK IN FINALLY (syntactic detection)
     TOOLS: bugbear/ruff (B012), pylint (W0150), CodeQL (py/exit-from-finally)
     NOTE: Syntactic DETECTION, but flow semantic effect.

  L. POINTLESS STATEMENT / USELESS EXPRESSION
     TOOLS: pylint (W0104), ruff (B018)
     FALSE POSITIVE RATE: MED

  M. REDUNDANT RE-RAISE (raise as first/only in except)
     TOOLS: pylint (W0706 try-except-raise)
     FALSE POSITIVE RATE: LOW

  N. REDEFINES LOOP VARIABLE INSIDE LOOP
     TOOLS: ruff (PLW2901), pylint (W2901)
     FALSE POSITIVE RATE: MED (augmented assignment false positive)

  O. MISSING EXCEPTION CHAIN (raise without from)
     TOOLS: pylint (W0707 raise-missing-from)
     FALSE POSITIVE RATE: LOW

  ============================================================
  RANKING FOR pflow check (review value × low false-positive)
  ============================================================
  Rank  Detector                          Classification  FP Rate
  1     use-before-def (possibly-unbound) FLOW-NATIVE     MED     — definite runtime error
  2     unreachable code                  FLOW-NATIVE     LOW     — dead code
  3     inconsistent/missing return       FLOW-NATIVE     LOW     — implicit None bugs
  4     resource leak (post-dominance)    FLOW-NATIVE     MED     — file/lock leaks
  5     None dereference (Optional)       FLOW-NATIVE     LOW*    — frequent runtime error (*needs types)
  6     exception swallowing (finally)    AST+Flow        LOW     — silent exception loss
  7     loop var post-loop (unbound)      FLOW-NATIVE     MED     — silent NameError
  8     unreachable except block          FLOW-NATIVE     LOW     — dead handler
  9     dead store (full liveness)        FLOW-NATIVE     LOW     — NOT YET in mainstream tools
  10    exception swallowing (pass)       AST-LINT        LOW     — silent error hiding
  11    loop variable capture (closures)  FLOW-NATIVE     LOW     — classic Python gotcha
  12    redundant condition               FLOW-NATIVE     MED     — logic errors
  
  Lower-priority (AST-LINT, easy to add):
  A  unused import                       AST-LINT        LOW
  B  unused variable (never-ref)         AST-LINT        LOW
  C  mutable default argument            AST-LINT        LOW
  D  comparison of constants             AST-LINT        VERY LOW
  E  redundant else after return         AST-LINT        LOW-MED
  F  useless expression                  AST-LINT        MED
<<<END>>>


SOURCE
id: 51
ts: 2026-05-29T11:15:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: Exact CFG/Dataflow Properties Required per Detector (Implementation Guide)
authors: (synthesis record)
year: 2026
venue: synthesis
quality: DOCS
url: (synthesis — based on all above sources)
keywords: implementation, CFG property, reaching definitions, post-dominance, liveness, def-use
cites: records 1-50
cited_by:
content:
  This record catalogs the exact flow-graph property needed per detector for a
  pflow check implementation using CFG + dominators/post-dominators +
  reaching-definitions + def-use chains.

  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  DETECTOR: USE-BEFORE-DEFINITION (possibly-unbound)
  PROPERTY: USE with no reaching DEF.
    Formally: there exists a path from entry to USE-node that does not pass through
    any DEF-node for the same variable.
  ALGORITHM: Reaching definitions analysis (classic forward dataflow).
    GEN[B] = {defs generated in B}
    KILL[B] = {prior defs killed by B's defs}
    IN[B] = ∩(predecessors P of B: OUT[P])  [must-reach: intersection join]
    A USE at statement s is possibly-unbound if s's IN set doesn't include a DEF for
    the variable. For DEFINITE unbound: use union join instead of intersection.
  PYTHON SPECIFICS: finally blocks need special handling — they have multiple incoming
    paths (normal + exception). Loop bodies may-not-execute (treat loop entry as
    conditional branch with empty-body option). Exception variable erasure (Python 3
    `except E as e` deletes `e` after the block) needs explicit DEL node in CFG.
  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  DETECTOR: UNREACHABLE CODE
  PROPERTY: CFG node (basic block) with no path from entry.
  ALGORITHM: Forward DFS/BFS from entry node. Mark all reachable nodes. Unmarked
    nodes are unreachable. All statements within unreachable blocks are dead code.
  NOTE: Type-narrowing-based unreachable (pyright) additionally requires propagating
    type lattice state; code is unreachable when type narrows to Never.
  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  DETECTOR: INCONSISTENT / MISSING RETURN
  PROPERTY: Exists a path from entry to function-exit that does not pass through
    a `return expr` statement.
  ALGORITHM: Backward analysis or forward analysis to find exit nodes.
    Find all terminal CFG nodes (no successors within function).
    For each terminal: does it correspond to a `return expr` or `raise`?
    If any terminal is implicit fall-through, flag.
    NoReturn annotations on called functions also terminate paths — must model.
  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  DETECTOR: DEAD STORE / ASSIGNMENT NEVER READ
  PROPERTY: DEF d with no USE u such that d reaches u (no live use downstream).
  ALGORITHM: Liveness analysis (classic backward dataflow).
    USE[B] = {names used in B before any def in B}
    DEF[B] = {names defined in B}
    OUT[B] = ∪(successors S of B: IN[S])
    IN[B] = USE[B] ∪ (OUT[B] - DEF[B])
    A DEF at statement s is dead if s's DEF name is not in OUT[s].
    Alternative: build def-use chains; a DEF with empty use-set is dead.
  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  DETECTOR: RESOURCE LEAK (acquire not post-dominated by release)
  PROPERTY: acquire op a such that NOT every path from a to function-exit passes
    through a corresponding release op.
  ALGORITHM: Post-dominator tree computation.
    Post-dominators: compute using reverse-CFG dominator tree.
    release post-dominates acquire iff release is in post-dom set of acquire.
    If release NOT in post-dom(acquire), there's a path from acquire to exit
    without release — potential leak.
  NOTE: Exception paths make this harder — must include exception-flow edges in CFG.
    Python's `with` statement adds an implicit guarantee: __exit__ called on all paths
    including exceptions. Model `with` as: __enter__ → body (with exception edge to
    __exit__) → __exit__ → continuation.
  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  DETECTOR: NONE DEREFERENCE / OPTIONAL MISUSE
  PROPERTY: USE of variable v where v has type T | None at USE point,
    and the USE operation is not valid on None.
  ALGORITHM: Type-state propagation over CFG.
    Type state: lattice where each variable has type T | None or T (narrowed).
    After `if x is not None:`, in the true branch x has type T.
    JOIN: union of types from all incoming edges (may-have-None on any path → type includes None).
    A reportOptional* fires when type at USE still includes None.
  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  DETECTOR: EXCEPTION SWALLOWING — FULL FLOW VERSION
  PROPERTY: except handler body has no path that executes a bare `raise` or `raise e`.
  ALGORITHM: Forward analysis of except body CFG.
    Check all exit paths from except handler.
    If no exit path passes through a raise statement, exception is swallowed.
    This is equivalent to: `raise` is NOT reachable from any except-handler terminal.
  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  DETECTOR: LOOP VARIABLE POSSIBLY-UNBOUND POST-LOOP
  PROPERTY: Variable defined ONLY in for-loop target (DEF inside loop), and READ
    after the loop exits, where the loop might not execute (empty iterable).
  ALGORITHM: Treat for-loop as: entry → (maybe branch to body) → (maybe branch to after).
    The "body doesn't execute" branch goes directly to after-loop.
    Using reaching defs with this model: after loop, the loop-variable def does NOT
    necessarily reach (since body might not run). Flag post-loop USE.
  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  DETECTOR: UNREACHABLE EXCEPT BLOCK (exception order)
  PROPERTY: except E2 handler where there exists a preceding except E1 handler such
    that E2 is a subclass of E1 (every E2 exception is also an E1 exception).
  ALGORITHM: For each pair of consecutive except handlers (E1, E2): check if E2 is
    a subclass of E1 in Python's MRO. If so, E2 handler is unreachable.
  DATA NEEDED: Exception class hierarchy (MRO lookup via type inference).
  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  DETECTOR: REDUNDANT CONDITION (via type narrowing)
  PROPERTY: Condition is always true or always false given the type state at that point.
  ALGORITHM: Type-state propagation over CFG. At the condition site, evaluate whether
    the condition can ever be False (for always-true) or True (for always-false) given
    the type lattice state. If type state implies the condition is constant, flag it.
    Special case: `isinstance(x, int) and isinstance(x, str)` — second isinstance
    is redundant given first (int and str share no common instances).
  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  DETECTOR: RETURN/BREAK IN FINALLY (exception suppression)
  PROPERTY: finally-block contains a control-flow escape (return/break/continue)
    on some path, which preempts exception propagation.
  ALGORITHM: For each finally block: check if any path through the finally block
    exits via return/break/continue (not via fall-through to end of finally).
    If so, any in-flight exception from the corresponding try/except is discarded.
    Basic check: is there a return/break/continue reachable from the start of the
    finally block before the end of the finally block?
<<<END>>>


